  "stdout": "# Advanced Neutron Source Collimation for Standoff Material Identification\n\n## Title\n**Neural-Field-Aperture Collimated Americium-Beryllium Source Assembly for Standoff Threat Detection**\n\n*A Novel Hierarchical Metamaterial Collimation and Resonant Neutron Transport Architecture*\n\n---\n\n## Background & Motivation\n\n### 1.1 Problem Statement\n\nCurrent man-portable neutron interrogation systems for standoff threat detection employ isotropic AmBe neutron sources (typically 10–1000 Ci activity) positioned within bulky shielding enclosures. While effective for localized inspection, isotropic emission profiles present three critical limitations:\n\n1. **Operator Dose Burden:** ≥95% of neutron flux traverses the operator position, requiring heavy rear shielding (typically 30–60 cm of borated polyethylene + lead) that renders systems impractical for sustained handheld operation (>15 kg total mass).\n\n2. **Signal-to-Noise Degradation:** Only ~5% of emitted neutrons contribute to the interrogation cone at standoff distances (1–5 m), resulting in prohibitively long acquisition times (>30 minutes for comparable results from a collimated source operating at <100 Ci).\n\n3. **SWaP-C Constraints:** The DARPA SIGMA+ program's Size, Weight, Power, and Cost requirements demand a radical departure from conventional heavy-shielding paradigms.\n\n### 1.2 Current State-of-the-Art Limitations\n\nTraditional neutron collimation approaches rely on:\n- **Absorptive collimation:** Thick layers of borated polyethylene with machined bore geometries—inefficient for fast neutrons due to elastic scattering that broadens the beam.\n- **Reflective collimation:** Simple tungsten or lead annular reflectors that redirect a portion of rear-emitted neutrons forward—but without spectral discrimination, leading to undesirable thermal contamination in the interrogation cone.\n- **Mozart-style channel collimators:** Long parallel bore assemblies effective only for thermal neutrons; fast neutron scattering within channels produces unacceptable angular divergence.\n\nThese conventional mechanisms are incremental at best and do not achieve the required **≥80% forward collimation into a ≤20° cone** while maintaining mass-to-flux ratios suitable for man-portable deployment.\n\n### 1.3 Revolutionary Opportunity\n\nThis proposal introduces a **tripartite novel architecture** combining:\n1. **Hierarchical Metamaterial Moderation (HMM):** A graded-index neutron moderator using functionally-graded composites that spatially vary hydrogen density and boron concentration to achieve velocity-dependent refraction analogous to GRIN (Gradient-Index) optical lenses.\n2. **Resonant Interference Collimation (RIC):** A phononic-crystal-inspired reflector assembly exploiting constructive/destructive interference patterns in epithermal neutron transport to create an angularly-selective passband.\n3. **Topologically-Optimized Backscatter Attenuation (TOBA):** An inverse-design rear shield leveraging density-topology optimization to minimize rear-hemisphere mass while maximizing Back-scatter Shielding Efficacy (BSE).\n\n---\n\n## Assumptions\n\n- **Source specification:** Standard AmBe source configuration yielding ~6×10⁷ n/s per Ci, with a Watt fission spectrum (E_max ≈ 11 MeV, mean ≈ 4.2 MeV).\n- **Target material class:** Conventional explosives (RDX, HMX, PETN) and illicit narcotics (fentanyl, methamphetamine) requiring interrogation at standoff distances of 1–5 m.\n- **Environmental conditions:** Operational temperatures from −20°C to +50°C; standard atmospheric pressure; no vacuum requirements.\n- **Regulatory framework:** Compliance with 10 CFR Part 71 (DOT/NRC packaging requirements for special form radioactive material), 49 CFR 173.465 (transport conditions), and IAEA Safety Standards Series No. SSR-6.\n- **Performance benchmark:** Material interrogation depth equivalent to 30 cm packed soil (ρ = 1.5 g/cm³, areal density = 4.5 g/cm²) with peak flux at 1–5 m standoff.\n\n---\n\n## Objectives\n\n### Primary Objective\nDevelop a computational model of a man-portable AmBe neutron source assembly achieving:\n\n| KPP | Target | Rationale |\n|-----|--------|-----------|\n| **Collimation Factor (Cf)** | ≥80% of emitted neutrons within ≤20° forward cone | Maximizes interrogation efficiency |\n| **Mass-to-Flux Ratio (MFR)** | < 2 kg/(n/s·sr) at standoff position | Enables man-portable deployment |\n| **Back-scatter Shielding Efficacy (BSE)** | > 95% rear-hemisphere attenuation | Minimizes operator dose |\n| **Required Source Activity** | ≤500 Ci (transportable as Special Form) | Regulatory compliance; manageable licensing |\n\n### Secondary Objectives\n\n1. **Maximize thermal neutron flux** at standoff distances of 1–5 m through spectral tailoring in the collimation assembly.\n2. **Demonstrate peak flux capability** at material depth equivalent to 30 cm packed soil (4.5 g/cm² areal density).\n3. **Propose dual-wall encapsulation protocol** for powdered AmBe source compliant with DOT/NRC special form requirements.\n4. **Achieve mass reduction** of rear-hemisphere shielding by ≥40% compared to conventional designs through topological optimization.\n\n---\n\n## Methodology / Technical Approach\n\n### 2.1 Computational Framework\n\n#### 2.1.1 Monte Carlo Transport Simulation\n\nPrimary simulations will utilize **MCNP6.2** (or **Geant4-DNA** extension) with the following configuration:\n\n```\nSource Definition:\n  - AmBe spectrum: Watt fission spectrum (α = 0.988 MeV⁻¹, β = 0.321 MeV⁻¹)\n  - 4π isotropic emission initially; subsequently collimated through geometry\n  - Energy bins: 10⁻⁵ eV (thermal) to 15 MeV (fast), logarithmic spacing\n\nGeometry Primitives:\n  - Spherical source capsule: r = 2.5 cm (nominal)\n  - HMM moderator shell: inner r = 3.0 cm, outer r = 12.0 cm\n  - RIC reflector assembly: r = 12.0 cm to r = 18.0 cm\n  - TOBA rear shield: r = 6.0 cm to r = 14.0 cm (rear hemisphere)\n  - Forward aperture: 20° half-angle cone, opening at r = 12.0 cm\n\nTallies:\n  - F5: Point detector flux at 1 m, 3 m, and 5 m standoff along beam axis\n  - F4: Cell flux in forward cone (≤20°) vs. full sphere\n  - FM card: Angular discrimination filters\n  - DE/DF cards: Energy binning for spectral analysis\n```\n\n#### 2.1.2 Validation Strategy\n\n",
